WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.373
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:10.605
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Let's

00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:13.580
start with a bit of
a visualization exercise.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.150
Who comes to mind when
I say the word coder?

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.460
Think of someone who can whip
up a quick computer program, or

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.320
knows multiple different
programming languages.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.440
Do you have someone in mind?

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.050
Maybe, okay.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.130
Who pictured Mark Zuckerberg?

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.510
Quick show of hands, anyone.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.551
Who pictured some other
Silicon Valley geeks?

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:39.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Me too.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.570
&gt;&gt; Who pictured Lyndsey Scott, she's
a top model, a Victoria's Secret Angel?

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.143
Yet, she's coded several
popular mobile apps.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:53.983
Who pictured Nji Collins Gbah?

00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:57.573
A 17 year old Cameroonian
boy who's self taught coder,

00:00:57.573 --> 00:01:01.392
yet just won a huge international
competition run by Google.

00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:10.040
One more question.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.374
Why didn't you picture yourself?

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:18.990
When it comes to coding we're
all plagued with self-doubt.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.630
I'm not good enough at math,
I can't get the syntax right.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.660
I'm way too old to start coding now.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.750
These are exactly the myths
I'm here to dispel.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.045
I'm confident you can learn how to code,

00:01:32.045 --> 00:01:34.732
and that your professional
life will be better for it.

00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:42.100
Regardless of whether you're an executive
assistant, senior executive, or

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.915
NBA student, you can make it through
the ups and downs and become a true coder.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.250
For me, self doubt was a familiar feeling.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:52.940
In the fall of 2015,

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.970
I enrolled in Stanford's intermediate
computer science class, CS 107.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.220
The class was over capacity, so

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.415
we met in an extra large auditorium
in the business school, Cemex.

00:02:03.415 --> 00:02:06.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The seats were filled with young

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.850
sophomores who waved their hands
at the professor, pick me,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:15.620
pick me, as they tried to
answer every single question.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.680
This is even more frightening
because computer science classes

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.445
do not have participation grades.

00:02:20.445 --> 00:02:25.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the back of the room there was me,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.170
the recovering investment banker.

00:02:28.776 --> 00:02:32.660
[LAUGH] Who felt ancient and
out of place in this crowd.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.600
Among Stanford's brightest geniuses,
I was really learning how to code for

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:38.369
the first time.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.600
The first assignments in
that class nearly broke me.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:49.200
It seemed at 3 AM every night,
I'd be reading through my code

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.180
trying to find that one bug, one error,
that was preventing it from running.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.113
I read, I searched.

00:03:00.751 --> 00:03:03.105
I questioned whether I
should just give up.

00:03:03.105 --> 00:03:07.229
Throw in the towel and go back to my old
job making pretty stock price charts.

00:03:07.229 --> 00:03:10.472
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; CS 107,

00:03:10.472 --> 00:03:15.070
that intermediate computer science
class ends with a big assignment.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:16.940
It's called the heap allocator.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.980
The heap both stores and retrieves data.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.918
The class assignment evaluated both
on accuracy and time benchmarks.

00:03:23.918 --> 00:03:30.180
I was convinced that I was going to
build the best heap that I could.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.020
I wanted to prove to myself,
I belonged in that class.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.390
So I set out over Thanksgiving
break to optimize my heap.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:39.320
Keep coding, keep trying.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.340
And I couldn't have been
more pleased by the results.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.710
If I were to compare my heap to an Olympic
hurdler, it not only would have been in

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:52.740
the final heap, but
it would have won a bright, shining medal.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.810
It ran through the assigned tasks
without making a single mistake.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.600
I couldn't have been prouder.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:06.290
All of those late nights were paying off,
I was becoming a true coder.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:14.174
A similar victory is available to you,
you can make it through the ups and

00:04:14.174 --> 00:04:19.657
downs and achieve an incredibly
worthy goal, becoming a coder.

00:04:22.792 --> 00:04:27.000
I've talked with a lot of my friends
at Stanford about learning how to code.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.260
Through those conversations I've
identified three common questions.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.480
The first common question is,
what really is coding?

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.135
Coding is how humans
boss computers around.

00:04:38.135 --> 00:04:43.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We often talk about writing code,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.660
but I like to think about
it as collecting code.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.310
There's so
many great programs online today.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.200
These can be combined together to create
something that's bigger than the sum

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:51.989
of its parts.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:53.800
What that means for

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.980
you, is that you can get a lot of mileage
out of a basic understanding of coding.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.450
You can stand on the shoulders of giants.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:04.400
And with the basic understanding use
the latest machine learning algorithms or

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.327
impress your friends with whatever
else you want to make up.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.680
The second question I'm often ask is,
why is this important?

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.050
Technology has had the power to
continuously improve our lives.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.970
Yet, our skill set has needed to keep up

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.670
to be able to take advantage
of those advances.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.765
For example,
think about typing like on a typewriter.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.670
In the 1950s only specialists
secretaries typed.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:37.730
They sat in the middle of the office and
processed everyone's documents.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:41.550
But time passed and in the 1980s
personal computers became prevalent.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.680
Then it was no longer sufficient for

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.170
just secretaries to type everyone
in the office needed to learn how.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.170
That's brought us to today where
typing is an omnipresent skill.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.140
I expect we'll go through the same
trajectory with with coding.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.860
We're already past the point
where only engineers, or

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.240
specialists in this case, code.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.065
Out of the top quartile
by ranked by salary,

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:10.080
50% already require some sort of coding or
technical skills.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.810
So that means that there
are the marketers, data scientists,

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:16.010
biz ops people.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.010
Those beyond engineers
who are already coding.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.700
To me it's inevitable that
this trend continues.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:26.512
That we'll reach a point where all
people in all roles are coding.

00:06:30.943 --> 00:06:36.020
You may be thinking to yourself at
this point, I'm going to be a manager.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.900
I'll have my engineers
to do all the coding.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.307
What a good wishful thinking.

00:06:42.307 --> 00:06:46.748
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To be able to

00:06:46.748 --> 00:06:50.910
effectively communicate with engineers,
an understanding of code is critical.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:55.340
It helps you speak the same language,
and be able to do your work faster and

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:55.980
more effectively.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:02.220
The third question I'm often asked is,
how do I get started?

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.140
We are incredibly fortunate
Stanford to have incredible

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.720
encoding resources available to us.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:16.240
It's as if we stumbled into Stanford's
Sistine Chapel of computer science.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.753
We can look up and
learn a ton from Michelangelo's ceiling.

00:07:20.753 --> 00:07:24.401
CS 106A one of the most
popular classes for

00:07:24.401 --> 00:07:27.963
MBA students is being offered this spring.

00:07:30.319 --> 00:07:32.630
Of course there are online resources too.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:36.800
One of my favorites is
Codecademy's Python class.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.990
In under ten hours you can go from knowing
nothing about Python, to having a solid

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.879
understanding of the language and
being able to build your own programs.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.350
You may be thinking at this point,
when am I going to make time to code.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.536
I can barely do my laundry as it is,
me too.

00:07:53.536 --> 00:07:56.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I completely understand.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.290
We have so many special
opportunities available to us here.

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:04.530
However, if you are at all interested in
coding, now is the best time to learn.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:08.980
We have access to unparalleled
resources at Stanford.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:13.952
CS classes are pass fail for MBA students,
and there will be more distractions.

00:08:13.952 --> 00:08:17.424
Whether that's starting a company or
starting a family post graduation.

00:08:17.424 --> 00:08:24.193
Picture yourself as a coder humor me
with one more visualization exercise.

00:08:24.193 --> 00:08:27.097
Come on, so picture yourself.

00:08:27.097 --> 00:08:31.340
It's a Wednesday afternoon you are sipping
on your favorite caffeinated beverage, you

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:37.890
are in the office and your laptop is
toiling away, taking care of your chores.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:43.206
Updating sales statistics,
scraping down customer data and

00:08:43.206 --> 00:08:47.098
then there is you doing
really what matters.

00:08:47.098 --> 00:08:49.290
You're mentoring a high
potential new employee, or

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:53.000
you're thinking through your
company's next strategic moves.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:58.290
You're doing the things you came to
Stanford GSP to learn how to do.

00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:59.560
What an incredible vision.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.560
Don't let the opportunity to
make this a reality slip by.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.860
With confidence in your own abilities
take a class and start coding.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.343
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:12.343 --> 00:09:16.529
[MUSIC]

